To automatically generate a table of contents in a LaTeX document, you can use the `\tableofcontents` command. This command collects sectioning commands (like `\section`, `\subsection`, etc.) and organizes them into a structured table of contents. Here’s an example:

```latex
\documentclass{article}

\begin{document}

\title{Sample Document}
\author{Your Name}
\date{\today}
\maketitle

% Generate the table of contents
\tableofcontents

\newpage

% Main content
\section{Introduction}
This is the introduction section.

\subsection{Background}
Some background information is provided here.

\section{Main Content}
This section contains the main content of the document.

\subsection{Subsection One}
Details of the first subsection.

\subsection{Subsection Two}
Details of the second subsection.

\section{Conclusion}
This is the conclusion section.

\end{document}
```

In this example:
1. The `\tableofcontents` command generates the table of contents.
2. Sectioning commands (`\section`, `\subsection`) are used to structure the document.
3. Use `\newpage` to ensure the table of contents appears on a separate page.

Compile the document twice to ensure all entries in the table of contents are correctly displayed. This approach ensures an organized and automatic table of contents.

